## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design an order-statistics structure with duplicates using a compact, well-known DS (Fenwick). Requires handling inserts, deletes, and k-th retrieval within tight bounds.

## Key Concepts

- Coordinate compression to bound domain size
- Fenwick Tree for prefix sums (counts)
- getKthSmallest via binary lifting on Fenwick
- Handling duplicates and safe deletes
- Time: O(log m) per operation, m distinct values

## Code Example

```javascript
class Fenwick {
  constructor(n){ this.n=n; this.f=new Array(n+1).fill(0); }
  add(i, delta){ for(; i<=this.n; i+= i&-i) this.f[i]+=delta; }
  sum(i){ let s=0; for(; i>0; i-= i&-i) s+=this.f[i]; return s; }
  kth(k){
    let idx=0; let bit=1;
    while((bit<<1) <= this.n) bit <<= 1;
    for(let step=bit; step>0; step>>=1){
      const t = idx+step;
      if(t<=this.n && this.f[t] < k){
        idx = t;
        k -= this.f[t];
      }
    }
    return idx+1;
  }
}
class KthMultiset {
  constructor(vals){
    this.values = Array.from(new Set(vals)).sort((a,b)=>a-b);
    this.index = new Map(this.values.map((v,i)=>[v,i+1]));
    this.ft = new Fenwick(this.values.length);
  }
  add(x){ const i = this.index.get(x); if(i) this.ft.add(i, 1); }
  remove(x){ const i = this.index.get(x); if(i) this.ft.add(i, -1); }
  getKthSmallest(k){ const idx = this.ft.kth(k); return this.values[idx-1]; }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Streaming sliding-window max is a practical problem in real-time data analysis. It tests the understanding of deques, amortized analysis, and handling duplicates in a fixed-size window.

## Key Concepts

- Monotonic deque for candidate maxima
- Fixed-size sliding window via index-based eviction
- Amortized O(1) per push/getMax
- Duplicate handling via index tracking

## Code Example

```javascript
class SlidingWindowMax {
  constructor(K) {
    this.K = K;
    this.dq = []; // stores indices
    this.values = [];
    this.i = 0;
  }
  push(x) {
    this.values[this.i] = x;
    while (this.dq.length && this.values[this.dq[this.dq.length - 1]] <= x) {
      this.dq.pop();
    }
    this.dq.push(this.i);
    if (this.dq[0] <= this.i - this.K) {
      this.dq.shift();
    }
    this.i++;
  }
  getMax() {
    return this.dq.length ? this.values[this.dq[0]] : null;
  }
  size() {
    return Math.min(this.i, this.K);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you modify for a variable window size?
- How would you adapt to support getMin() alongside getMax()?

Why This Is Asked
- Tests dynamic graph state and a non-trivial at-query metric.

Key Concepts
- Dynamic adjacency via hash sets, on-demand intersection, edge-id tracking, amortized performance.

Code Example
```javascript
class DynamicGraph {
  constructor(){ this.adj = new Map(); this.edges = new Map(); }
  addEdge(u,v,id){ if(!this.adj.has(u)) this.adj.set(u,new Set()); if(!this.adj.has(v)) this.adj.set(v,new Set()); this.adj.get(u).add(v); this.adj.get(v).add(u); this.edges.set(id,[u,v]); }
  removeEdge(id){ const e = this.edges.get(id); if(!e) return; const [u,v] = e; this.adj.get(u).delete(v); this.adj.get(v).delete(u); this.edges.delete(id); }
  twoStepPaths(u,v){ if(u===v) return 0; const A = this.adj.get(u) || new Set(); const B = this.adj.get(v) || new Set(); if(A.size < B.size){ let c=0; for(const x of A) if(B.has(x)) c++; return c; } else { let c=0; for(const x of B) if(A.has(x)) c++; return c; } }
}
```

Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to directed graphs or multi-edges? Impact on correctness and complexity?
- What if you need to maintain this metric for many (u, v) pairs offline at once—any batching strategies?
